{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778596885604,"sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9dbe9010056d6549","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"progenitor cell"},"output":"Found 2921 matches (showing first 100)\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json:\n  Line 2:   \"body\": \"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nUnclassified Fallopian Tube Progenitor (UCFP)\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\nPrior work (PMID: 35320732) has referred to similar UCFP cells as clusters 2-1 and 2-2 that can give rise to epithelial and endothelial/stromal cell types through scRNA-seq and trajectory inference.\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\nWe propose a new, broad definition for the observed \\\"dual-feature\\\" human fallopian tube progenitor-like cells that simultaneously express epithelial (e.g., EpCAM/CD326) and endothelial (e.g., PECAM1/CD31) markers, both on the cell surface and by RNA expression that can give rise to ciliated and secretory cell types, and potentially to endothelial/stromal cell types. These \\\"dual-feature\\\" unclassified fallopian tube progenitor cells have primary been observed by single-cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq), flow cytometry, and immunofluorescence, with the UCFP term proposed in PMID: 40475517.\\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\nI am not entirely sure as these could exist under CL:4052019 or CL:4052018. \\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\nHuman fallopian tube\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\n0000-0002-1482-1032\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\nI'm sure this is incomplete and is my first attempt at this :) Thank you and my apologies in advance!\\n\\n\",\n\n  Line 10:       \"body\": \"Looks good, but would prefer something like 'dual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell' as a name. I guess 'unclassified' is historical at this point - you just classified it.\",\n\n  Line 25:       \"body\": \"Discussion with Ben: He is happy with us using `dual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell` as the main label in CL.\",\n\n  Line 40:       \"body\": \"**Updates on text and logical defs (have been reviewed by @biobenkj ):**\\n\\n**label**\\ndual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell\\n\\n**textual definition**\\n\\nA bipotent progenitor cell within the human fallopian tube epithelium, characterized by the concurrent expression of epithelial (e.g., EpCAM/CD326) and endothelial (e.g., PECAM1/CD31) markers at both the cell surface and transcript levels. This cell has the capacity to differentiate into ciliated and secretory epithelial cells, as well as potentially endothelial/stromal lineages. Positioned at the apex of lineage bifurcation, the cell exhibits stem-like and endothelial features, representing an intermediate developmental state between undifferentiated progenitors and lineage-committed epithelial cells.\\n\\n PMID: [40475517](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40475517/)\\n\\n**Synonyms**\\n\\nnon-ciliated secretory epithelial cell 2-1 (NCSE2-1 cells) - related synonym -  PMID: [35320732](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35320732/)\\nnon-ciliated secretory epithelial cell 2-2 (NCSE2-2 cells) - related synonym -  PMID: [35320732](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35320732/)\\nunclassified fallopian tube progenitors - exact-  PMID: [40475517](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40475517/)\\n\\n**Logical definition:**\\n\\n'progenitor cell'\\n'part of' some 'fallopian tube epithelium'\\n'in taxon' some 'Homo sapiens'\\n'develops into' some 'fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell'\\n'develops into' some 'fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell'\\n\",\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/templates/blood_and_immune_upper_slim.csv:\n  Line 4: CL:0000038,http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl#blood_and_immune_upper_slim,erythroid progenitor cell\r\n\n  Line 26: CL:0000837,http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl#blood_and_immune_upper_slim,hematopoietic multipotent progenitor cell\r\n\n  Line 31: CL:0002031,http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl#blood_and_immune_upper_slim,hematopoietic lineage restricted progenitor cell\r\n\n  Line 32: CL:0002032,http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl#blood_and_immune_upper_slim,hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell\r\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/dosdp-patterns/cellBearerOfQuality.yaml:\n  Line 5: #examples: CL_0000413 haploid cell, CL_0000225 anucleate cell, CL_0000227 binucleate cell, CL_0002032 'hematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell', CL_0001064 'malignant cell' (25 total)\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/matches/cellCapableOfBiologicalProcess.tsv:\n  Line 16: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000051\tcommon lymphoid progenitor\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030098\tlymphocyte differentiation\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002032\thematopoietic oligopotent progenitor cell\r\n\n  Line 30: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002191\tgranulocytopoietic cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030851\tgranulocyte differentiation\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000839\tmyeloid lineage restricted progenitor cell\r\n\n  Line 37: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000838\tlymphoid lineage restricted progenitor cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030098\tlymphocyte differentiation\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002031\thematopoietic lineage restricted progenitor cell\r\n\n  Line 40: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002194\tmonopoietic cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030224\tmonocyte differentiation\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000839\tmyeloid lineage restricted progenitor cell\r\n\n  Line 43: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000839\tmyeloid lineage restricted progenitor cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030099\tmyeloid cell differentiation\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002031\thematopoietic lineage restricted progenitor cell\r\n\n  Line 45: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000834\tneutrophil progenitor cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030223\tneutrophil differentiation\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002191\tgranulocytopoietic cell\r\n\n  Line 57: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000827\tpro-T cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0030217\tT cell differentiation\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000838\tlymphoid lineage restricted progenitor cell\r\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/definitions.owl:\n  Line 291: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1016/j.cell.2019.08.053\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1016/j.conb.2022.102674\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1016/j.tins.2021.11.001\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.1038/nri3086\") Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"DOI:10.3389/fnins.2021.742065\") <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000129> \"Microglial cells, sometimes referred to as microglia, are a type of glial cell that primarily exist within the central nervous system (CNS), notably in the brain and the spinal cord. Classified among the resident immune cells, microglial cells represent about 10% of all cells found within the CNS. These cells are derived from progenitor cells in the yolk sac, which differentiates them from other types of glial cells (such as astrocytes and oligodendrocytes) that are derived from neuroectodermal cell lineages. \n\n  Line 423: Annot