{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778598441877,"sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a3a0c4f1be1a4d72","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"fallopian tube"},"output":"Found 80 matches\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json:\n  Line 2:   \"body\": \"Please check that the term does not already exist by using the ontology search tool OLS:\\nhttps://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl\\n\\n\\n**Preferred term label**\\nUnclassified Fallopian Tube Progenitor (UCFP)\\n\\n**Synonyms** (add reference(s), please)\\nPrior work (PMID: 35320732) has referred to similar UCFP cells as clusters 2-1 and 2-2 that can give rise to epithelial and endothelial/stromal cell types through scRNA-seq and trajectory inference.\\n\\n**Definition** (free text, with reference(s), please. PubMed ID format is PMID:XXXXXX)\\nWe propose a new, broad definition for the observed \\\"dual-feature\\\" human fallopian tube progenitor-like cells that simultaneously express epithelial (e.g., EpCAM/CD326) and endothelial (e.g., PECAM1/CD31) markers, both on the cell surface and by RNA expression that can give rise to ciliated and secretory cell types, and potentially to endothelial/stromal cell types. These \\\"dual-feature\\\" unclassified fallopian tube progenitor cells have primary been observed by single-cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq), flow cytometry, and immunofluorescence, with the UCFP term proposed in PMID: 40475517.\\n\\n**Parent cell type term** (check the hierarchy here https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/cl)\\nI am not entirely sure as these could exist under CL:4052019 or CL:4052018. \\n\\n**Anatomical structure where the cell type is found** (check Uberon for anatomical structures: https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/uberon)\\nHuman fallopian tube\\n\\n**Your ORCID**\\n0000-0002-1482-1032\\n\\n**Additional notes or concerns**\\nI'm sure this is incomplete and is my first attempt at this :) Thank you and my apologies in advance!\\n\\n\",\n\n  Line 10:       \"body\": \"Looks good, but would prefer something like 'dual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell' as a name. I guess 'unclassified' is historical at this point - you just classified it.\",\n\n  Line 25:       \"body\": \"Discussion with Ben: He is happy with us using `dual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell` as the main label in CL.\",\n\n  Line 40:       \"body\": \"**Updates on text and logical defs (have been reviewed by @biobenkj ):**\\n\\n**label**\\ndual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell\\n\\n**textual definition**\\n\\nA bipotent progenitor cell within the human fallopian tube epithelium, characterized by the concurrent expression of epithelial (e.g., EpCAM/CD326) and endothelial (e.g., PECAM1/CD31) markers at both the cell surface and transcript levels. This cell has the capacity to differentiate into ciliated and secretory epithelial cells, as well as potentially endothelial/stromal lineages. Positioned at the apex of lineage bifurcation, the cell exhibits stem-like and endothelial features, representing an intermediate developmental state between undifferentiated progenitors and lineage-committed epithelial cells.\\n\\n PMID: [40475517](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40475517/)\\n\\n**Synonyms**\\n\\nnon-ciliated secretory epithelial cell 2-1 (NCSE2-1 cells) - related synonym -  PMID: [35320732](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35320732/)\\nnon-ciliated secretory epithelial cell 2-2 (NCSE2-2 cells) - related synonym -  PMID: [35320732](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35320732/)\\nunclassified fallopian tube progenitors - exact-  PMID: [40475517](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40475517/)\\n\\n**Logical definition:**\\n\\n'progenitor cell'\\n'part of' some 'fallopian tube epithelium'\\n'in taxon' some 'Homo sapiens'\\n'develops into' some 'fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell'\\n'develops into' some 'fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell'\\n\",\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/definitions.owl:\n  Line 784: Multi-ciliated epithelial cells have fundamental roles in the proper functioning of many organ systems. In the respiratory system, they line the airways and orchestrate the coordinated movement of mucus, effectively clearing the airways of inhaled particles and pathogens. These cells are also vital in the ventricular system of the brain where they facilitate cerebrospinal fluid circulation, thus contributing to the maintenance of the brain's microenvironment. In the fallopian tube, multi-ciliated epithelial cells aid in the transport of oocytes from the ovary to the uterus, a process crucial to reproduction.\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/default/ExtendedDescription.tsv:\n  Line 455: Multi-ciliated epithelial cells have fundamental roles in the proper functioning of many organ systems. In the respiratory system, they line the airways and orchestrate the coordinated movement of mucus, effectively clearing the airways of inhaled particles and pathogens. These cells are also vital in the ventricular system of the brain where they facilitate cerebrospinal fluid circulation, thus contributing to the maintenance of the brain's microenvironment. In the fallopian tube, multi-ciliated epithelial cells aid in the transport of oocytes from the ovary to the uterus, a process crucial to reproduction.\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/reports/release-diff.md:\n  Line 34501: ### fallopian tube `http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0003889`\n\n  Line 34503: - [fallopian tube](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0003889) SubClassOf [in taxon](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002162) some [Mammalia](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_40674) \n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/cl-refactored.owl:\n  Line 17498: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"CALOHA:TS-1277\"^^xsd:string) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1001591> \"fallopian tube glandular cells\"^^xsd:string)\n\n  Line 17500: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"CALOHA:TS-1277\"^^xsd:string) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1001591> \"fallopian tube glandular cell\"^^xsd:string)\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/cl-edit_neg_exp.owl:\n  Line 81904:         <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">fallopian tube glandular cell</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\n\n  Line 81905:         <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">fallopian tube glandular cells</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\n\n  Line 81916:         <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">fallopian tube glandular cell</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 81928:         <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">fallopian tube glandular cells</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl:\n  Line 28446: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1277\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001591 \"fallopian tube glandular cell\")\n\n  Line 28447: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1277\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001591 \"fallopian tube glandular cells\")\n\n  Line 31222: # Class: obo:CL_4030006 (fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 31224: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1186/1477-7827-7-129\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells8080933\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030006 \"An epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube that secretes mucus and oviduct-specific products in response to hormonal stimulation from estrogen and luteinizing hormone. This fallopian tube secretory cell is similar in height to the ciliated cell, but typically exhibits a more narrow, columnar shape. Its nucleus is ovoid and oriented perpendicular to the cell's long axis, with denser chromatin and a smaller nucleolus compared to the ciliated cell.\")\n\n  Line 31229: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccr.2013.10.013\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030006 \"The other cell types of the fallopian tube epithelium include the ciliated cell and the intercalary cell, or peg cell. The fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell type has been implicated as a source of high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma.\")\n\n  Line 31230: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4030006 \"fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 31235: # Class: obo:CL_4030007 (fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 31237: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16565155\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/humanproteome/tissue/fallopian+tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030007 \"A multi-ciliated epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube, mainly found on the apex of the mucosal folds. This cell exhibits a columnar shape with an oval nucleus and is characterized by the presence of cilia on its surface. The coordinated beating of these cilia, together with peristaltic contractions, contributes to the self-propulsion of spermatozoa, the transport of ovum during ovulation and the transport of the fertilized ovum to the intramural fallopian tube.\")\n\n  Line 31241: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube ciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 31242: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube multiciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 31244: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 31974: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2210508\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30023181\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033014 \"A small, narrow, peg-shaped epithelial cell with little cytoplasm that is part of oviduct epithelium. This cell is rarer than the ciliated and secretory epithelial cells of the fallopian tube epithelium and is often found intercalated between them. Peg cells are generally distributed basally along the epithelium and have been found in high concentrations at the fimbriated, distal end of the fallopian tube in humans. It may have a regenerative/stem-cell function. In humans, markers include EPCAM, CD44, and ITGA6.\")\n\n  Line 31979: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4033014 \"fallopian tube epithelial stem-like cell\")\n\n  Line 33829: # Class: obo:CL_4052018 (fallopian tube epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 33831: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Fallopian_tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052018 \"Any epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube.\")\n\n  Line 33836: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052018 \"fallopian tube epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 33839: # Class: obo:CL_4052019 (fallopian tube non-ciliated epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 33841: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052019 \"Any epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube and lacks cilia.\")\n\n  Line 33844: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4052019 \"fallopian tube non-ciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 33845: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052019 \"fallopian tube non-ciliated epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 33874: # Class: obo:CL_4052022 (fallopian tube smooth muscle cell)\n\n  Line 33876: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31183831\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31613440\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052022 \"A smooth muscle cell that is part of the fallopian tube. This cell is responsible for peristaltic contractions that facilitate gamete and embryo transport, fluid mixing, and embryo admission to the uterus.\")\n\n  Line 33881: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18621753\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4052022 \"The fallopian tube smooth muscle cell in the human contracts dynamically in response to hormones and signaling molecules. Prostaglandins, particularly PGF2α and PGE2, have been shown to increase muscular contractions, while progesterone, levonorgestrel, mifepristone, oxytocin, and human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) decrease them. These contractile responses are crucial for regulating gamete and embryo transport through the fallopian tube.\")\n\n  Line 33882: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052022 \"fallopian tube smooth muscle cell\")\n\n  Line 34315: # Class: obo:CL_4052070 (dual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell)\n\n  Line 34317: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:40475517\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052070 \"A bipotent progenitor cell within the human fallopian tube epithelium, characterized by the concurrent expression of epithelial (e.g., EpCAM/CD326) and endothelial (e.g., PECAM1/CD31) markers at both the cell surface and transcript levels. This cell has the capacity to differentiate into ciliated and secretory epithelial cells, as well as potentially endothelial/stromal lineages. Positioned at the apex of lineage bifurcation, the cell exhibits stem-like and endothelial features, representing an intermediate developmental state between undifferentiated progenitors and lineage-committed epithelial cells.\")\n\n  Line 34320: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:40475517\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4052070 \"unclassified fallopian tube progenitor\")\n\n  Line 34323: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052070 \"dual-feature fallopian tube progenitor cell\")\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/for_reference/Manchester_CL_normalisation_expt/owl/go.owl:\n  Line 377780:             >fallopian tube development</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 377785:             >The reproductive developmental process whose specific outcome is the progression of the fallopian tubes over time, from their formation to the mature structures.</def>\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/patterns/definitions.owl:\n  Line 784: Multi-ciliated epithelial cells have fundamental roles in the proper functioning of many organ systems. In the respiratory system, they line the airways and orchestrate the coordinated movement of mucus, effectively clearing the airways of inhaled particles and pathogens. These cells are also vital in the ventricular system of the brain where they facilitate cerebrospinal fluid circulation, thus contributing to the maintenance of the brain's microenvironment. In the fallopian tube, multi-ciliated epithelial cells aid in the transport of oocytes from the ovary to the uterus, a process crucial to reproduction.\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/patterns/data/default/ExtendedDescription.tsv:\n  Line 455: Multi-ciliated epithelial cells have fundamental roles in the proper functioning of many organ systems. In the respiratory system, they line the airways and orchestrate the coordinated movement of mucus, effectively clearing the airways of inhaled particles and pathogens. These cells are also vital in the ventricular system of the brain where they facilitate cerebrospinal fluid circulation, thus contributing to the maintenance of the brain's microenvironment. In the fallopian tube, multi-ciliated epithelial cells aid in the transport of oocytes from the ovary to the uterus, a process crucial to reproduction.\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/reports/release-diff.md:\n  Line 34501: ### fallopian tube `http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0003889`\n\n  Line 34503: - [fallopian tube](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0003889) SubClassOf [in taxon](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002162) some [Mammalia](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCBITaxon_40674) \n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-refactored.owl:\n  Line 17498: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"CALOHA:TS-1277\"^^xsd:string) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1001591> \"fallopian tube glandular cells\"^^xsd:string)\n\n  Line 17500: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(<http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDbXref> \"CALOHA:TS-1277\"^^xsd:string) <http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym> <http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1001591> \"fallopian tube glandular cell\"^^xsd:string)\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-edit_neg_exp.owl:\n  Line 81904:         <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">fallopian tube glandular cell</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\n\n  Line 81905:         <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">fallopian tube glandular cells</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>\n\n  Line 81916:         <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">fallopian tube glandular cell</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 81928:         <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:datatype=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\">fallopian tube glandular cells</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/src/ontology/cl-edit.owl:\n  Line 28445: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1277\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001591 \"fallopian tube glandular cell\")\n\n  Line 28446: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"CALOHA:TS-1277\") oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym obo:CL_1001591 \"fallopian tube glandular cells\")\n\n  Line 31221: # Class: obo:CL_4030006 (fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 31223: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1186/1477-7827-7-129\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells8080933\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030006 \"An epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube that secretes mucus and oviduct-specific products in response to hormonal stimulation from estrogen and luteinizing hormone. This fallopian tube secretory cell is similar in height to the ciliated cell, but typically exhibits a more narrow, columnar shape. Its nucleus is ovoid and oriented perpendicular to the cell's long axis, with denser chromatin and a smaller nucleolus compared to the ciliated cell.\")\n\n  Line 31228: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccr.2013.10.013\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/learn/dictionary/normal/fallopian+tube\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030006 \"The other cell types of the fallopian tube epithelium include the ciliated cell and the intercalary cell, or peg cell. The fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell type has been implicated as a source of high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma.\")\n\n  Line 31229: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4030006 \"fallopian tube secretory epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 31234: # Class: obo:CL_4030007 (fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 31236: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16565155\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://www.proteinatlas.org/humanproteome/tissue/fallopian+tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030007 \"A multi-ciliated epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube, mainly found on the apex of the mucosal folds. This cell exhibits a columnar shape with an oval nucleus and is characterized by the presence of cilia on its surface. The coordinated beating of these cilia, together with peristaltic contractions, contributes to the self-propulsion of spermatozoa, the transport of ovum during ovulation and the transport of the fertilized ovum to the intramural fallopian tube.\")\n\n  Line 31240: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube ciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 31241: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube multiciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 31243: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4030007 \"fallopian tube multiciliated epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 31973: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:2210508\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30023181\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033014 \"A small, narrow, peg-shaped epithelial cell with little cytoplasm that is part of oviduct epithelium. This cell is rarer than the ciliated and secretory epithelial cells of the fallopian tube epithelium and is often found intercalated between them. Peg cells are generally distributed basally along the epithelium and have been found in high concentrations at the fimbriated, distal end of the fallopian tube in humans. It may have a regenerative/stem-cell function. In humans, markers include EPCAM, CD44, and ITGA6.\")\n\n  Line 31978: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22911892\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4033014 \"fallopian tube epithelial stem-like cell\")\n\n  Line 33828: # Class: obo:CL_4052018 (fallopian tube epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 33830: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"Wikipedia:Fallopian_tube\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052018 \"Any epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube.\")\n\n  Line 33835: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052018 \"fallopian tube epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 33838: # Class: obo:CL_4052019 (fallopian tube non-ciliated epithelial cell)\n\n  Line 33840: AnnotationAssertion(obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052019 \"Any epithelial cell that is part of the fallopian tube and lacks cilia.\")\n\n  Line 33843: AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4052019 \"fallopian tube non-ciliated cell\")\n\n  Line 33844: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052019 \"fallopian tube non-ciliated epithelial cell\")\n\n  Line 33873: # Class: obo:CL_4052022 (fallopian tube smooth muscle cell)\n\n  Line 33875: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31183831\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31613440\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052022 \"A smooth muscle cell that is part of the fallopian tube. This cell is responsible for peristaltic contractions that facilitate gamete and embryo transport, fluid mixing, and embryo admission to the uterus.\")\n\n  Line 33880: AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:18621753\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4052022 \"The fallopian tube smooth muscle cell in the human contracts dynamically in response to hormones and signaling molecules. Prostaglandins, particularly PGF2α and PGE2, have been shown to increase muscular contractions, while progesterone, levonorgestrel, mifepristone, oxytocin, and human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) decrease them. These contractile responses are crucial for regulating gamete and embryo transport through the fallopian tube.\")\n\n  Line 33881: AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052022 \"fallopian tube smooth muscle cell\")\n\n\n/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/for_reference/Manchester_CL_normalisation_expt/owl/go.owl:\n  Line 377780:             >fallopian tube development</rdfs:label>\n\n  Line 377785:             >The reproductive developmental process whose specific outcome is the progression of the fallopian tubes over time, from their formation to the mature structures.</def>\n","metadata":{"matches":80,"truncated":false},"title":"fallopian tube","time":{"start":1778598441836,"end":1778598441875}},"id":"prt_e1cba7369001nkTcg2Ypv4w4bV","sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","messageID":"msg_e1cba22dd001B4tGYx2Uf5dinY"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1778598441918,"sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","part":{"id":"prt_e1cba73bb0010bdjitUrBCyu8z","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"581d56d936aced0510c66e2319d300deede1adb9","messageID":"msg_e1cba22dd001B4tGYx2Uf5dinY","sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":82639,"input":81731,"output":908,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":0}},"cost":0.0168002}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1778598474654,"sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","part":{"id":"prt_e1cbaf39c0016ekst1Z4PVaFzq","messageID":"msg_e1cba73e0001U3G676Jyvab0M1","sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","snapshot":"581d56d936aced0510c66e2319d300deede1adb9","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778598615788,"sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-87fa9862eee7471a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/docs/relations_guide.md"},"output":"<path>/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/docs/relations_guide.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Cell Ontology (CL) relations guide.\n2: \n3: \n4: ## Intro \n5: The aim of this document is to provide an accessible guide to how to use relations to record the properties that define cell types including location, lineage, function, morphology and marker genes. The term 'relations' here refers principally to OWL object properties, but also includes annoation properties used as shortcuts for more expressive logical axioms that can be programatically generated from them.\n6: \n7: Relations in this guide are grouped by general use case (e.g. recording location) and each is illustrated by an example e.g.-\n8: \n9: -   melanocyte subClassOf ‘has part’ some melanosome.\n10: \n11: This should be read as ‘**all** melanocytes have some type of melanosome as a part’ as should all axioms of this form. The examples should all be correct, but may not reflect the full complexity of axioms in the ontology. Where no example is currently present in CL, examples are taken from the Drosophila Anatomy Ontology, which follows the same schema.\n12: \n13: ## Recording location\n14: \n15: Location of cell types is recorded by relating a cell type to a term in an anatomical ontology. For the Cell Ontology this means a term from Uberon. \n16: \n17: ###  [**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050) \n18: \n19: Use part\\_of for cases where the location is a material anatomical structure (rather than a space, such as a sinus) and all of the cell is within the anatomical structure.\n20: \n21: ‘[epithelial cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000066)\n22: subClassOf [**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050)\n23: *some* [epithelium](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000483)\n24: \n25: ‘part of’ is transitive, which means that it applies across chains of relationships. For example,\n26: \n27: ‘ileal goblet cell’ part\\_of some ileum\n28: \n29: ilium ‘part of’ some ‘small intestine’\n30: \n31: ‘small intestine’ ‘part of’ some intestine\n32: \n33: =>\n34: \n35: ’ileal goblet cell’ ‘part of’ some ‘small intestine’\n36: &\n37: ‘ileal goblet cell’ ‘part of’ some intestine\n38: \n39: ### located\\_in\n40: \n41: To record the location of a cell in an anatomical space (e.g., a sinus),\n42: [**'located in'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001025) is used.\n43: \n44: For example:\n45: \n46: ‘[lymph node marginal reticular cell](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0009103)’ subClassOf [**'located in'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0001025) *some* ['subcapsular sinus of lymph node'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0005463)\n47: \n48: ### overlaps\n49: \n50: [**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050) applies in cases where an entire cell is within an anatomical structure, but some cells have parts in multiple anatomical structures. For example, many neurons span multiple regions of the central nervous system. The general relation for this is [**overlaps**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002131) (has some part in).\n51: \n52: [**overlaps**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002131) is not currently used directly in the cell ontology (time of writing 05/2023), but more specific relationships exist for recording the location of neurons and their parts. These are described in the next section.\n53: \n54: ### Recording the location of neurons \n55: \n56: ### [<u>has soma location</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100)\n57: \n58: When neurobiologists talk about the location of vertebrate neurons, they are typically referring to soma location. The importance of soma location to identify is underscored by how commonly cell types are named, in part, by soma location. We therefore have a dedicated relation for recording this: [**'has soma location'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100).\n59: \n60: For example, [anterior horn motor neuron](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_2000048) has the following subclass axiom:\n61: \n62: [**'has soma location'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002100) *some* ['ventral horn of spinal cord'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002257)\n63: \n64: **axiomatization of ‘has soma location’**\n65: \n66: -   *subPropertyOf*: overlaps \\# if X has\\_soma\\_location some Y, then X overlaps some Y)\n67: \n68: -   *domain*: neuron \\# X has\\_soma\\_location some Y => X is inferred to be a subClassOf neuron\n69: \n70: -   *property chain*: has\\_soma\\_location o part\\_of --> has\\_soma\\_location \\# If x has soma location y and y is part\\_of z, then x has\\_soma\\_location\\_z\n71: \n72: **Example of reasoning with the property chain:**\n73: \n74: 'cortical interneuron' equivalentTo 'interneuron' that has\\_soma\\_location some 'cerebral cortex' \n75: \n76: 'rosehip neuron' subClassOf interneuron  and has\\_soma\\_location some 'cortical layer 1' \n77: \n78: 'cortical layer 1'  subClassOf part\\_of some 'cerebral cortex\n79: \n80: => 'rosehip neuron' subClassOf 'cortical interneuron'\n81: \n82: ### sends synaptic output to region\n83: \n84: A relationship between a neuron and a region, where the neuron has a functionally relevant number of output synapses in that region.\n85: \n86: '[<u>adult basket subesophageal neuron</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00051856)' SubClassOf [<u>sends synaptic output to region</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0013003)\n87: *some* [<u>inferior posterior slope</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00045046)\n88: \n89: ### receives synaptic input in region\n90: \n91: A relationship between a neuron and a region, where the neuron has a functionally relevant number of input synapses:\n92: \n93: e.g. '[<u>adult basket subesophageal neuron</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00051856)' SubClassOf ‘[<u>receives synaptic input in region</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0013002)*’\n94: some ‘*<u>[superior posterior slope](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/FBbt_00045040)’</u>\n95: \n96: ### fasciculates\\_with\n97: \n98: Use this to record the tracts or nerves that a neuron’s projections fasciculate with.\n99: \n100: e.g. ‘Betz cell’ subClasssOf ‘fasciculates with’ some ‘corticospinal tract’.\n101: \n102: subPropertyOf: overlaps\n103: \n104: domain: neuron\n105: \n106: range: neuron projection bundle\n107: \n108: ## Recording synaptic connectivity (neurons)\n109: \n110: To record neuron-to-neuron or motor neuron-to-target muscle connectivity, consider the following object properties. These properties should be used when connectivity is key to the definition, for example, in cases where a motor neuron type is defined by the type of muscle fiber on which it synapses.\n111: \n112: ### [<u>synapsed to</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002120)\n113: \n114: For example, ['alpha motor neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008038) SubClassOf [**synapsed to**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002120) *some* ['extrafusal muscle fiber'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008046)\n115: \n116: ### [<u>synapsed by</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002103)\n117: \n118: -   This is the the inverse of [**synapsed to**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002120)\n119: \n120: For example, ['extrafusal muscle fiber'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008046) SubClassOf [**synapsed by**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002103) *some* ['alpha motor neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0008038)\n121: \n122: ## Recording function\n123: \n124: Cellular function is recorded by linking GO biological process terms with the object properties [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) and ‘capable of part of’\n125: \n126: ###  [<u>'capable of'</u>](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215)\n127: \n128: Use this relation where the cell is capable of carrying out the entirety of the process\n129: \n130: For example, ['hilus cell of ovary'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002095) has the following subclass:\n131: \n132: [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) *some* ['androgen secretion'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0035935)\n133: \n134: ### Recording neurotransmitter for neurons\n135: \n136: To record which neurotransmitter a neuron releases, use a [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) relation to link the neuron to a subclass of GO [neurotransmitter secretion](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0007269) that references a neurotransmitter type. This should be sufficient for autoclassification.\n137: \n138: e.g. ['medium spiny neuron'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_1001474) [**'capable of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002215) some ['gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0061534)\n139: \n140: ### ‘capable of part of’\n141: \n142: Use this relationship where only part of the process occurs in the cell type.\n143: \n144: e.g.  'retinal bipolar neuron' 'capable of part of' some 'visual perception'\n145: \n146: ## Recording developmental lineage\n147: \n148: Developmental lineage is recorded between cell types with the object property [**develops from**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002202) (a transitive property), or in the case where there are no intermediates between the cells, [**'directly develops from'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002207) (a non-transitive subproperty of **develops_from**)\n149: \n150: For example, ['leukocyte'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000738) subClassOf [**develops from**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002202) *some* ['hematopoietic stem cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000037)\n151: \n152: ## Recording cell markers\n153: \n154: Only markers that are necessary to define a cell type should be recorded.\n155: \n156: ### cell surface (protein) markers\n157: \n158: The cell ontology has a set of terms for recording cell surface markers.\n159: \n160: The most commonly used relation for recording markers is [**'has plasma\n161: membrane part'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002104). This\n162: object property is used to record cell surface markers, especially in\n163: immune cells. There are also more specific properties, [**'has low\n164: plasma membrane amount'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0015016)\n165: and [**'has high plasma membrane\n166: amount'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0015015), that can be used\n167: at an editor's discretion. In each case, a term from the [PRotein\n168: Ontology (PRO)](https://github.com/PROconsortium/PRoteinOntology) or a\n169: protein complex term from the [Gene Ontology\n170: (GO)](https://github.com/geneontology/go-ontology) is used as the object\n171: of the relation.\n172: \n173: For example, ['alpha-beta T cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000789) has the following\n174: equivalence axiom:\n175: \n176: ['T cell'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000084) *and* [**'has\n177: plasma membrane part'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002104)\n178: *some* ['alpha-beta T cell receptor complex'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0042105)\n179: \n180: Absence of a marker can be recorded using\n181: [**lacks\\_plasma\\_membrane\\_part**](https://ontobee.org/ontology/CL?iri=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2Fcl%23lacks_plasma_membrane_part)\n182:  \n183: \n184: Warning - this is used in place of the more accurate OWL expression \"NOT has_part some X*** in order to keep within the EL profile of OWL. It's use with a general class as a target can potentially lead to reasoning errors.  \n185: \n186: ### recording gene markers\n187: \n188: ### ‘expresses’\n189: \n190: Use this to link a cell type to a gene or gene product that defines it:\n191: \n192: For example:\n193: \n194: 'lamp5 GABAergic cortical interneuron' EquivalentTo:\n195: interneuron and ('has soma location' some 'cerebral cortex')\n196: and ('capable of' some 'gamma-aminobutyric acid secretion, neurotransmission') \n197: and (expresses some 'lysosome-associated membrane glycoprotein 5')\n198: \n199: In FBbt, FlyBase Gene IDs are permitted here (using standard resolvable URL pattern).  In CL currenly only PRO IDs are permitted.  In PCL, a broader range of IDs have been used (depending on data sources used).\n200: \n201: ### Recording cell parts\n202: \n203: To record parts above the granularity of proteins and complexes, use a 'has part' relationship with an object from the Gene Ontology cellular_component branch.\n204: \n205: e.g. 'melanocyte' subClassOf 'has part' some 'melanosome'\n206: \n207: This GO term can be combined with a PATO quality term (e.g. for shape) where necessary, e.g.\n208: \n209: For example:\n210: \n211: 'mature basophil' subClassOf ('has part' some (nucleus and ('has characteristic' some lobed)))\n212: \n213: ## Recording general cellular characteristics\n214: \n215: The ontology [<u>PATO</u>](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/pato),\n216: has a rich set of terms that can be used to record the general\n217: characteristics of cells, such as their morphology. These are recorded\n218: using [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053).\n219: \n220: In choosing PATO terms, avoid those referring to some change in\n221: characteristic (e.g,.’ increased branchiness’). The following list of\n222: examples is not exhaustive:\n223: \n224: ### Recording Morphology\n225: \n226: PATO has a set of general morphology terms which may be applicable to\n227: cells.\n228: \n229: For example, [erythrocyte](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000765)\n230: subClassOf [**'has characteristic'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053)\n231: *some* [biconcave](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002039)\n232: \n233: PATO also has a set of terms for [<u>specific cell morphologies</u>](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ols4/ontologies/pato) (mostly\n234: neuronal), e.g.\n235: \n236: ‘Betz cell’ subClassOf ‘has characteristic’ some ‘standard pyramidal morphology’\n237: \n238: ### Recording nuclear number \n239: \n240: To record the number of nuclei in a cell, use a PATO subclass\n241: under the term ['nucleate quality'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001404) with the ['has\n242: characteristic'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) relation.\n243: \n244: ![image](https://github.com/obophenotype/cell-ontology/assets/112839/4b6aa141-c42f-4bff-9d9e-bdb71a17e882)\n245: \n246: For example, \n247: \n248: [platelet](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000233) subClassOf (['has_characteristic'](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000053) *some* [**anucleate**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001405))\n249: \n250: Note - that PATO includes bridging axioms that infer part relationships\n251: based on these characteristics.\n252: \n253: e.g.\n254: \n255: cell and ('has characteristic' some multinucleate) SubClassOf 'has part'\n256: some nucleus\n257:                        \n258: ## Taxon constraints\n259: \n260: See\n261: <https://oboacademy.github.io/obook/explanation/taxon-constraints-explainer/>.\n262: \n\n(End of file - total 262 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Cell Ontology (CL) relations guide.\n\n\n## Intro \nThe aim of this document is to provide an accessible guide to how to use relations to record the properties that define cell types including location, lineage, function, morphology and marker genes. The term 'relations' here refers principally to OWL object properties, but also includes annoation properties used as shortcuts for more expressive logical axioms that can be programatically generated from them.\n\nRelations in this guide are grouped by general use case (e.g. recording location) and each is illustrated by an example e.g.-\n\n-   melanocyte subClassOf ‘has part’ some melanosome.\n\nThis should be read as ‘**all** melanocytes have some type of melanosome as a part’ as should all axioms of this form. The examples should all be correct, but may not reflect the full complexity of axioms in the ontology. Where no example is currently present in CL, examples are taken from the Drosophila Anatomy Ontology, which follows the same schema.\n\n## Recording location\n\nLocation of cell types is recorded by relating a cell type to a term in an anatomical ontology. For the Cell Ontology this means a term from Uberon. \n\n###  [**'part of'**](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050) \n\nUse part\\_of for cases where the location is a material anatomical structure (rather than a space, such as a sinus) and all of the cell is within the anatomical structure.\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"docs/relations_guide.md","time":{"start":1778598615778,"end":1778598615786}},"id":"prt_e1cbcffc7001nMmpQrxZ5IyOsr","sessionID":"ses_1e365ad2bffe5HRu2su8GtgBuO","messageID":"msg_e1cba73e0001U3G676Jyvab0M1"}}
